// March Brown Spider: the wingless March Brown, hare's ear and gold under a turn of brown partridge, North Country style.

id march-brown-spider
name "March Brown"
variant-of march-brown
variant "Spider"
aliases "March Brown Spider"
type wet
imitates mayfly
naturals mayfly-emerger mayfly-nymph-crawling
difficulty beginner
species trout grayling
season march april may june
origin region="Northern England"
description "A slim hare's ear body ribbed with gold wire under a collar of brown partridge, with an orange silk head."
history """
    The hackled March Brown is as old as the winged one: Pritt (1885) gives one hackled with a
    woodcock feather, "very useful on cold, rough days". The spider tied today, in Sylvester
    Nemes's The Soft-Hackled Fly (1975) and Andrew Overend's list of North Country spiders, has
    no wing or tail: hare's face fur ribbed with narrow gold, a brown partridge hackle and
    orange silk.
    """
fishing """
    Upstream or across on a short line, in the film or just under it, during the March Brown
    hatch and through the spring; the soft partridge breathes in the current like the legs of a
    hatching dun. Often fished in a team with other spiders.
    """
sources {
    "The Soft-Hackle Pattern Book: March Brown Spider, Nymph or Flymph" url="http://softhacklepatternbook.blogspot.com/2014/03/march-brown-spider-nymph-or-flymph-and.html"
    "Hawker-Overend: How to tie and fish North Country spider flies (Andrew Overend)" url="https://hawker-overend.com/how-to-tie-and-fish-north-country-spider-flies/"
    "Internet Archive: Yorkshire Trout Flies (T. E. Pritt, 1885)" url="https://archive.org/details/yorkshiretroutfl00pritrich"
    "The Fly Tier's Page: March Brown Spider (Philip Bailey)" url="https://www.flytierspage.com/pbailey/march_brown_spider.htm"
}
// Photos for checking proportions while authoring (pnpm refcheck); never published.
references {
    "https://hawker-overend.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/March-brown-spider-1.jpg" page="https://hawker-overend.com/how-to-tie-and-fish-north-country-spider-flies/" author="Andrew Overend" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Side view in the vise"
    "https://www.flytierspage.com/pbailey/march_brown_spider.jpg" page="https://www.flytierspage.com/pbailey/march_brown_spider.htm" author="Philip Bailey" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Side view; a herl body, for the hackle only"
}

hook wet-standard size=14
thread orange size="8/0"

materials {
    rib gold-wire
    body hares-ear-dubbing
    hackle partridge-hackle fibre=1.7 sweep=40 label="Brown partridge back feather"
}

steps {
    attach-thread at=0.88
    // Read from the photos: the body starts about above the point.
    wrap-thread to=0.15
    tie-in rib
    dub body to=0.85 note="Thin and sparse."
    wind rib to=0.85 turns=4
    tie-off rib
    tie-in hackle note="By the tip, fluff stripped."
    wrap-thread to=0.92
    wind hackle to=0.92 turns=2 note="One or two turns, stroking the barbs back."
    tie-off hackle
    whip-finish note="A small orange head."
}
